Make #2117's new codec-profile default reach an installation that is upgraded.

tvheadend/tvheadend#2117, ours, one hunk set on src/config.c.  Offered there;
drop this patch once it is upstream.

The PR defaults a codec profile's `profile` field to FF_AV_PROFILE_UNKNOWN
(-99) instead of 0, so that 0 can be an explicit choice again -- it is Baseline
for nvenc (nvenc.c) and v4l2m2m (v4l2-m2m.c), both of which list it.  The
default is assigned in tvh_codec_profile_create() before idnode_load(), which
then overwrites it from the saved config, and saved configs do carry the field:
all 13 files under var/codec/ on a DSM install held "profile": 0, webtv-aac
included.  So the new default only ever applied to a profile created from
scratch, never to one loaded from disk -- which is every profile on every
existing installation.

A fresh install does not show it, which is why it reads as working:
data/conf/transcoder/codecs carries no "profile" field at all, so a profile
created from it keeps -99 and is saved with it.  The stored zeros are the work
of earlier versions, where the field defaulted to 0 and idnode_save() wrote it
out -- the upgrade path, which is the one every existing user is on.

config_migrate_v26 rewrites a stored "profile": 0 to -99 across codec/*.  A
one-shot migration rather than a test in the load path, so a user who does
select profile 0 afterwards is obeyed; the rewrite is lossless by construction,
since under the previous "profile > 0" code a stored 0 was never emitted.
Migrations run from config_init() ahead of codec_init(), so the codec subsystem
reads the rewritten files.

Verified on the NAS this package targets: the log shows the v25 to v26 step,
all 13 profiles moved from 0 to -99, no profile= option reaches the encoders
and the aac stream opens, while a profile set explicitly does reach h264_qsv
and is accepted.

Two things this patch used to carry are now in uknunknown's branch, so they
come with patch 100 instead: gating the aac Main and LTP profiles on
LIBAVCODEC_VERSION_MAJOR < 62 (his own fix, and the better one -- ffmpeg 8
removed both coders, so naming that keeps them on ffmpeg 7 where they still
encode), and the tidy-up of tvh_codec_profile_base_open().

--- src/config.c.orig
+++ src/config.c
@@ -1514,6 +1514,38 @@ config_migrate_v25 ( void )
   }
 }
 
+/*
+ * v25 -> v26 : codec profile "profile": 0 used to mean "unset"
+ *
+ * Every codec profile config written before the AV_PROFILE_UNKNOWN default
+ * carries "profile": 0 -- not a choice, just the zeroed field.  The encoder
+ * options were built with a "profile > 0" test, so a stored 0 behaved exactly
+ * as "no profile": the encoder picked its own.  Now that an explicit 0 is
+ * honoured (it is a real profile for some encoders), those stored zeros would
+ * start reaching the encoder and change, or break, existing streams.  Rewrite
+ * them to AV_PROFILE_UNKNOWN, which is the behaviour they have always had; a
+ * user who does want profile 0 can select it again and now be obeyed.
+ *
+ * -99 is spelled out because this file builds without libavcodec.
+ */
+static void
+config_migrate_v26 ( void )
+{
+  htsmsg_t *c, *e;
+  htsmsg_field_t *f;
+
+  /* Codec profiles are loaded after the migrations, so rewrite them on disk. */
+  if ((c = hts_settings_load("codec")) == NULL)
+    return;
+  HTSMSG_FOREACH(f, c) {
+    if (!(e = htsmsg_field_get_map(f))) continue;
+    if (htsmsg_get_s32_or_default(e, "profile", -99) != 0) continue;
+    htsmsg_set_s32(e, "profile", -99);
+    hts_settings_save(e, "codec/%s", htsmsg_field_name(f));
+  }
+  htsmsg_destroy(c);
+}
+
 /*
  * Perform backup
  */
@@ -1640,7 +1672,8 @@ static const config_migrate_t config_migrate_table[] = {
   config_migrate_v22,
   config_migrate_v23,
   config_migrate_v24,
-  config_migrate_v25
+  config_migrate_v25,
+  config_migrate_v26
 };
 
